**Theorem 9.1 (one common attainment of every required identity).**
Each construction in §8 is a normalized actual instrument with full
TP decoders. Its forgotten complete five-mode channel is exactly
$\widehat\Phi_*$ on every complex matrix and every passive reference,
with each original labelled Borel-time measure preserved separately.
It attains $E_*$, $\mathsf C(s)$, $\mathcal T(s)$ and the total
alphabet asserted in TA.39 simultaneously.

Proof. In the weak construction the low coefficient Gram is
$\operatorname{diag}(\delta,1,1)$ and the single high matrix adds
$\operatorname{diag}(1-\delta,0,0)$. In the strong construction,
summing the four low and two high coefficient projectors in TA.12
gives $I_3$: their three diagonal masses sum to one and all cross
moments cancel. Each image decoder reconstructs its exact $K_i$.
Absorption leaves its live product $K$ unchanged by TA.46.
Consequently the full live map for every complex $A\in M_5$ is

$$
\Phi^L(A)=E[DA_VD^\dagger+TA_VT^\dagger+SA_VS^\dagger]E^\dagger.
\tag{TA.48}
$$

The two spectral effects, including any absorbed $B_+$, sum to $M$.
Construction 8.2 adds exactly the complement effect in the canonical
failure sector. Thus for every Borel $B\subseteq[0,1]$ and every
original $\ell\in\{0,1,2,3,13\}$,

$$
\Phi^f_\ell(A)(B)=
 \left(\int_B\frac{2\,dt}{(1+t)^2}\right)
 [w_\ell\operatorname{tr}(MA_V)
    +\mathbf1_{\ell=2}\operatorname{tr}((I_5-P)A)]P_f.
\tag{TA.49}
$$

The sums of encoder effects are exactly

$$
E(D^\dagger D+T^\dagger T+S^\dagger S+M)E^\dagger+(I_5-P)=I_5.
\tag{TA.50}
$$

Absorption preserves this sum by TA.44–46. Individual finite effects
are positive and bounded by $I_5$, hence each encoder is CP/TNI.
Range decoders are TP on all $M_2$, ordinary high decoders on all
$M_3$, the enlarged high decoder on all $M_4$, and the absorbed
decoder on all $M_2$ by TA.46. Scalar failure decoders are TP because
$\sum_\ell w_\ell=1$ and $\int_0^1g(t)dt=1$.

These are identities of Kraus conjugations and trace functionals on
complex matrix units. Cross-support operators between $P$ and $I-P$
are killed by the specified encoder Kraus matrices; all extra-sector
maps vanish. Original labels $1,2,3$ stay separate, labels $0,13$
stay zero, and there is no singular or endpoint-atom addition.
Common metadata keep their original fixed state. For a joint operator
$X$ and arbitrary passive reference $R$, tensor all Kraus identities
with $I_R$. The amplified live output is

$$
\sum_{K\in\{D,T,S\}}(EKE^\dagger\otimes I_R)X
                              (EK^\dagger E^\dagger\otimes I_R).
\tag{TA.51}
$$

The two traces in TA.49 become the exact conditional reference operators

$$
\Theta_M(X)=\operatorname{Tr}_5[(EME^\dagger\otimes I_R)X],\qquad
\Theta_\perp(X)=\operatorname{Tr}_5[((I_5-P)\otimes I_R)X].
\tag{TA.52}
$$

The amplified labelled output is
$[\int_Bg]P_f\otimes[w_\ell\Theta_M(X)+\mathbf1_{\ell=2}\Theta_\perp(X)]$.
No conditional reference state is replaced by an unconditional marginal.
This proves every labelled Borel identity at every amplification.
The actual $g$ has not replaced the ideal $f_y$: equality to the same
complete approximate channel is what preserves all twelve original
HALF full-cq errors and their maximum $E_*$.

The actual laws are explicit for all twelve histories. An ordinary
low label has mass $\|K_iU_{e,n}a_y\|^2$; an unabsorbed spectral
label has mass $|b_\epsilon U_{e,n}a_y|^2$. At absorption their one
